15.3 Ledger-entry rules
A ledger entry must:
identify its source;
preserve exact technical identifiers where known;
distinguish fact from report, inference, and proposal;
avoid silently replacing another conversation's contribution;
link to durable artifacts where possible;
record disagreements rather than hiding them;
state what later agents need to verify;
remain append-oriented during concurrent Round 1 recovery.
15.4 Implementation completion rule
For ViewTube implementation work, an item is not considered a completed task merely because an audit, plan, proposal, or code change was discussed.
Where implementation status is being tracked, completion requires the applicable implementation to be actually completed and, for project task/quick-win purposes, merged to main and verified.
Audits and analysis remain valuable recovery evidence, but they are not themselves implementation completion unless the task definition explicitly says otherwise.
15.5 Recovery lineage
When an artifact or implementation is reconstructed from another source, record its lineage:
SOURCE → DISCOVERY → DECISION → IMPLEMENTATION → VERIFICATION
Where a stage is unavailable, mark it UNKNOWN rather than inventing it.
For recovered/donor code or reconstructed repositories, preserve:
donor/source repository;
source commit or revision when known;
files copied or reconstructed;
modifications made after recovery;
verification performed;
unresolved divergence from the original project;
whether the result is canonical, compatibility-only, historical, or unverified.
15.6 Cross-conversation handoff rule
A conversation must leave enough information in the shared ledger and durable artifacts that another conversation can continue without access to the original chat.
The minimum handoff is:
WHAT WAS FOUND → WHERE IT CAME FROM → WHAT IT MEANS → WHAT IS VERIFIED → WHAT IS NOT → WHAT MUST HAPPEN NEXT
